From: accel-virt
Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: target-independent rasterization, emulated exactly

Direct3D 11.1's target-independent rasterization (a rasterizer state's
ForcedSampleCount above 1 with single-sample render targets bound, or with
none) rasterizes at the forced count whatever the targets are: the pixel
shader runs once for each pixel with any sample of D3D's standard pattern
covered and sees that pattern's coverage as SV_Coverage. Direct2D draws its
anti-aliased geometry this way - 16 samples, the masks OR'd or XOR'd into an
R16_UINT atlas and counted - and DXVK ignored the forced count with a
render target bound, so the atlas got one sample's coverage and hairlines
lost rows. Vulkan rasterizes at the attachments' sample count (only NVIDIA's
mixed samples decouple the two), and RADV rasterizes 8 samples at most.

It is emulated, the same way on every GPU. The draw is rasterized at one
sample, conservatively, so every pixel the triangle touches gets a fragment.
A generated geometry shader passes the vertex or domain shader's outputs on
and hands the pixel shader its triangle in the rasterizer's own fixed point
(16.8, round-to-nearest-even, from viewport 0 in push data), with the edges'
top-left flags and each clip distance over w. A variant of the pixel shader
computes the forced pattern's coverage from it in 64-bit integers - every
edge function positive, or zero on a top or left edge - and clips at every
sample, replaces SV_Coverage with it, interpolates by sample index at the
forced pattern's positions, and demotes a pixel with no sample covered.
UAV-only rendering at a forced count takes the same path: the hardware's
geometric clipping at clip distances moved an edge past a sample where D3D
clips each sample. sampleinfo reports the forced count as the rasterizer's.

Not emulated, drawn as before: lines and points, wireframe, a geometry
shader or stream output of the application's, a previous stage writing the
layer or viewport index. A triangle with a vertex at w <= 0 or past 2^21
pixels counts every sample of each pixel it touches.
---
